        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Ellipticals</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. The picks were made from makers' specification tables,
            retailer listings, warranty terms and independent coverage, all checked on 19 October 2026.
            To qualify, a machine had to be under about 60 inches long, on sale under its current name,
            and backed by an independent source for its stride, capacity and footprint. We favored
            machines sold on Amazon or by a maker with its own support line.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recall checks.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
            recall database by brand for every maker on this page — Schwinn, BowFlex and their owner
            Johnson Health Tech, Niceday and FitNation (Echelon) — and for ellipticals as a product type.
            Nothing current covers any machine listed here, and we found no elliptical recall of any brand
            since 2018. Older ones are worth knowing if you buy used: Nautilus, which then owned the Schwinn
            brand, recalled Schwinn elliptical equipment in December 2011; Johnson Health Tech recalled
            Horizon and LIVESTRONG ellipticals in January 2010 and Matrix Ascent Trainers and ellipticals
            in 2014, re-announced in 2018. Johnson Health Tech's recalls of BowFlex <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>adjustable dumbbells</a> (June 2025) and Matrix training cycles (January 2025) do not cover ellipticals. Check the serial
            number of any second-hand machine against the CPSC database before you pay.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>SOLE E25.</strong> BarBend lists it as a compact elliptical with a high weight
