        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best compact elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most small spaces, the Schwinn 411: 53.8 inches long, an 18-inch stride, a 300 lb capacity and a published ceiling figure of your height plus 17 inches, for $699. For the shortest full-intensity machine, the BowFlex Max Trainer M6 is about 46 inches long; for a full 20-inch stride, the Schwinn 490 is 58.1 inches.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best elliptical for small spaces?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually a short one rather than a folding one. The Schwinn 411 is the best all-round choice; the FitNation Flex is about 37 inches long for the tightest rooms, with a shorter stride and a 260 lb limit. Check the ceiling as well as the floor, because compact machines often lift the pedals higher.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much ceiling height do I need for an elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Your height plus the maker's pedal-height figure, plus a few inches of headroom. Schwinn gives your height plus 17 inches for the 411 and plus 28.3 inches for the 490. A 6 ft user needs 89 inches on the 411 but 100.3 inches on the 490, which is more than a standard 8 ft ceiling.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What stride length do I need on an elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Reviewers treat 18 to 20 inches as standard for most adults. Users under about 5 ft 6 in. often manage with 16 to 18 inches; taller users usually want 20. A stride that is too short feels choppy rather than dangerous. Compact machines range from about 15 inches to the Schwinn 490's 20 inches.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are compact ellipticals stable?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The heavier ones are. A short, light frame has less mass and a smaller base, and reviewers reported some shifting on budget machines. Place it on a firm, level surface or dense mat, retighten bolts after the first sessions, and choose a capacity rating well above your weight, preferably one an independent reviewer confirms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the BowFlex Max Trainer an elliptical?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a hybrid. Its pedals move on a steep, stair-climbing path with moving handles, so it feels more like a stepper than a gliding elliptical. Reviewers treat it as the leading compact elliptical because it is short and delivers hard intervals, but if you want a traditional elliptical motion, choose the Schwinn 411 or 490.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
